{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791195262,"nonce":"I0zL8YaoEDvKF+HeUNJVfW4lprg=","message":"Fixed, with findings that change how the report reads.\n\n\nThe renderer corrected for fallback glyphs after layout, by accumulating an\noffset while it walked the glyphs, using the RASTERIZER's rounded advance\nagainst the layout's float advance. Layout, line breaking and measurement\nnever saw it. So a run measured at one width, wrapped at the wrong places and\ndrew at another.\n\n\nNot reproduced with emoji under the system face. SF Pro's missing-glyph advance\nhappens to be exactly 1em, which is also the emoji face's advance, so they\nagreed by luck. It shows with any primary whose notdef differs. Measured at\n16px, drawn minus measured:\n\n    monospace   emoji, heart, bolt   +6.37px each\n    serif       emoji, heart, bolt   +3.55px each\n    system      U+2605 (black star)  -6.25px   (the primary HAS the glyph,\n                                       but the renderer treats it as emoji)\n\n\nOne new module, blinc_text::fallback, and one call after shaping. A\nFallbackFaces value (symbol and emoji faces) substitutes the fallback glyph and\nadvance into the shaped run, converted into the PRIMARY face's font units\nbecause every consumer scales by the primary's units_per_em. Every path starts\nfrom that shaped run, so widths, wrapping, caret stops and drawing all read the\nsame advances without touching the three places that compute them. Each glyph\nrecords which face it is from, so the renderer rasterises the right one and no\noffset accumulates.\n\nRules are the renderer's, unchanged: whitespace, variation selectors and\njoiners are left alone; emoji try the emoji face then the symbol face, anything\nelse the symbol face; the symbol face loads when any character needs a\nfallback and the large emoji face only when an emoji is present. ASCII text\nneeds nothing and skips the registry lock.\n\nTextLayoutEngine::layout keeps its signature and behaves exactly as before.\nlayout_with_fallbacks takes the faces, and FallbackFaces::resolve loads them\nfor a string. FontTextMeasurer and the renderer use them.\n\n\nhl_blinc_text_carets calls TextLayoutEngine::layout. Switch it to\nlayout_with_fallbacks with FallbackFaces::resolve(\u0026mut registry, \u0026primary, text)\nand each PositionedGlyph's x and face are what the renderer draws.\n\n\n- 15 primary and glyph pairs: layout width equals where the renderer puts the\n  next glyph, within 0.5px, including every case that was off by 3 to 6px.\n- A run of six emoji in a 40px column wraps to 3 lines with fallback and 2\n  without; the old advance packed them too tightly.\n- The measurer agrees with the renderer, and ASCII measures exactly as the\n  plain engine does.\n- Both fixes were mutated and the tests failed each time.\n- ASCII scenes are pixel-identical before and after (0 differing pixels in\n  the baseline capture and in the rich text editor).\n- Rendered original against fixed for monospace and system rows: in the\n  original the text overflows its box and repeats are blank gaps; fixed, every\n  emoji draws and each box fits.\n\n\nstyled text (prepare_styled_text) has never drawn through a fallback face and\nstill does not. A CJK character has no fallback face in the chain at all and\ndraws the primary's missing-glyph box; that is a separate gap, not this one.","files":null}]}